## Section 1 — Architecture review

Four findings. Walking them one at a time.

**Issue 1 — `[P1] (confidence: 8/10)` the save debounce can starve to never firing.**

The plan specifies *"**Debounce 1s** (`DispatchWorkItem`, cancel-and-replace)"* on a sink over `terminals.objectWillChange`. But the publisher fires faster than 1 Hz whenever a terminal is doing anything:

```swift
// TerminalSessions.swift:262
@Published private(set) var activityPulses: [TerminalTab.ID: Int] = [:]

// TerminalSessions.swift:1100-1102
func pulsePaneActivity(_ tabID: TerminalTab.ID) {
  activityPulses[tabID, default: 0] += 1
}

// TerminalSessions.swift:1131 — title/cwd/progress path
/// Mutate the `.terminal` payload of a tab in place and republish (the `@Published`-driving reassign).
private func mutateTerminalState(...)
```

Cancel-and-replace means every publish pushes the deadline out. A sustained stream at >1/s (a build streaming output, a `claude` session animating its title — the plan's own text says 10×/s) resets the timer forever and **the write never lands**. The digest guard doesn't save you: it runs *inside* the work item that never executes.

**Issue 3 — `[P2] (confidence: 9/10)` a second, parallel projection of `TabContent`.**

The plan adds `TabSession` as a terminal-excluding projection of `TabContent`. One already exists, and its doc comment states the invariant a second one puts at risk:

```swift
// TerminalSessions.swift:124-128
// MARK: - Navigation-history bridge
//
// The `TabContent` → history projections live here, next to `TabContent`, so a fifth content kind is a
// compile error in ONE place instead of failing silently in several. Both switches below are
// exhaustive on purpose — no `default` arm.
```

Add a fifth content kind and you now have two places to remember, in two files.

**Issue 4 — `[P2] (confidence: 9/10)` two sources of truth for window frames, and the existing one is ungated across windows.**

The per-window frame observer is installed for *every* window and writes one shared key, with no `persistsSidebarPrefs` gate:

```swift
// AppStore.swift:990-999
for name in [NSWindow.didResizeNotification, NSWindow.didMoveNotification] {
  let token = NotificationCenter.default.addObserver(
    forName: name, object: window, queue: .main
  ) { [weak window] _ in
    guard let window else { return }
    MainActor.assumeIsolated { Defaults[.mainWindowFrame] = NSStringFromRect(window.frame) }
  }
```

Today that's a minor wart (three windows fight over one key; the last mover wins). Once the session file also stores a frame per window, the same value has two homes writing on the same events — the failure mode the codebase already calls out for `NavPayload` (*"leaving them here would give one value two homes"*, `TerminalSessions.swift:135`).

